<h2>Wallpaper vs Alternatives</h2>
<h3>Wallpaper vs Wall Panels (PVC / WPC)</h3>
<p>PVC and WPC wall panels at ₹80–₹200/sq ft installed offer a 3D relief effect that wallpaper cannot match. However, they add 8–12mm thickness to the wall and require clean straight walls. For TV unit walls, panels often deliver a better finish than wallpaper at comparable cost. For large rooms, wallpaper offers more design flexibility.</p>
<h3>Wallpaper vs Texture Paint</h3>
<p>Texture paint (₹30–₹80/sq ft) creates surface relief but offers no pattern or photographic capability. For budget renovations where only texture (not pattern) is needed, texture paint is faster and cheaper. For any design beyond basic texture, wallpaper dominates.</p>
<h3>Wallpaper vs Laminates</h3>
<p>Laminates are rigid sheets applied over board surfaces — they cannot be applied directly to plastered walls. Laminate is a furniture surface product; wallpaper is a wall surface product. Comparing them applies only in the context of cabinet backs and false panels, where laminates outperform on durability.</p>
<h3>Wallpaper vs Stone / Brick Cladding</h3>
<p>Natural stone or brick wall cladding costs ₹200–₹800/sq ft versus stone-effect wallpaper at ₹80–₹200/sq ft. The wallpaper delivers 70–80% of the visual result at 20–40% of the cost and 100% of the installation speed. For a rented property or a single accent wall, wallpaper is the unambiguous choice.</p>
